The combined rate, the part of the job they complete in \(2\) days, and the remaining work are computed below. Worker B's rate is \(\frac{1}{12}\), so the last line gives the time needed:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{combined rate} &= \frac{1}{8}+\frac{1}{12}=\frac{3}{24}+\frac{2}{24}=\frac{5}{24}\\
\text{work done together} &= 2\cdot\frac{5}{24}=\frac{10}{24}=\frac{5}{12}\\
\text{remaining work} &= 1-\frac{5}{12}=\frac{7}{12}\\
\text{time for B} &= \frac{\frac{7}{12}}{\frac{1}{12}}=7\text{ days}
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore B needs \(7\) more days.

Let B's time alone be \(x\) days. Then, subtracting \(\frac{1}{10}\) from both sides:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{10}+\frac{1}{x} &= \frac{1}{6}\\
\frac{1}{x} &= \frac{1}{6}-\frac{1}{10}=\frac{5}{30}-\frac{3}{30}=\frac{2}{30}=\frac{1}{15}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus \(x=15\). Therefore B alone takes \(15\) days.

Let the other tap's time be \(x\) minutes. Then, subtracting \(\frac{1}{20}\) from both sides:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{20}+\frac{1}{x} &= \frac{1}{12}\\
\frac{1}{x} &= \frac{1}{12}-\frac{1}{20}=\frac{5}{60}-\frac{3}{60}=\frac{2}{60}=\frac{1}{30}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus \(x=30\). Therefore the other tap takes \(30\) minutes alone.

Let the rates of A, B, and C be \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\). Then \(a+b=\frac{1}{12}\), \(b+c=\frac{1}{15}\), and \(a+c=\frac{1}{20}\). Adding these equations and using a common denominator of \(60\) gives the first line. Thus \(a+b+c=\frac{1}{10}\). Since \(b+c=\frac{1}{15}\), we get the second line:
\[
\begin{aligned}
2(a+b+c) &= \frac{1}{12}+\frac{1}{15}+\frac{1}{20}=\frac{5}{60}+\frac{4}{60}+\frac{3}{60}=\frac{12}{60}=\frac{1}{5}\\
a &= \frac{1}{10}-\frac{1}{15}=\frac{3}{30}-\frac{2}{30}=\frac{1}{30}
\end{aligned}
\]
So A alone takes \(30\) days.

A's rate is \(\frac{1}{10}\), and B's rate is \(\frac{1}{15}\). The work A completes in the first \(3\) hours, the remaining work, the combined rate when both work together, and the time needed to finish the remaining work are:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{work by A alone} &= 3\cdot\frac{1}{10}=\frac{3}{10}\\
\text{remaining work} &= 1-\frac{3}{10}=\frac{7}{10}\\
\text{combined rate} &= \frac{1}{10}+\frac{1}{15}=\frac{3}{30}+\frac{2}{30}=\frac{5}{30}=\frac{1}{6}\\
\text{remaining time} &= \frac{\frac{7}{10}}{\frac{1}{6}}=\frac{7}{10}\cdot 6=\frac{42}{10}=4.2\text{ hours}
\end{aligned}
\]
Thus the total time is \(3+4.2=7.2\) hours, which is \(7\) hours \(12\) minutes.
